                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "34607da3-4b6d-499f-8f65-4f82cc62b853",
                        "source_entry_id": "fb5a7bf9-6f2a-3826-9b39-e9ba1c1f880d",
                        "source_sense_id": "647ccc1e-5d94-395f-ac44-93ba1cbd6092",
                        "target_lang_word": "semiprime",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "semiprime",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "42f89954-2596-3729-ab12-eee33625033a",
                                        "entry_id": "55bf388d-a9fa-3094-8eda-15d59f43f014",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A natural number that is the product of two (not necessarily distinct) prime numbers."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1be01914-fc50-496d-99af-9f83a35d63d7",
                                                "sense_id": "42f89954-2596-3729-ab12-eee33625033a",
                                                "text": "Again, to be perfectly clear, we are looking for c values that produce a low density of semiprimes when employing Euler's basic polynomial but changing the c values, in the range of x=1 to 10000. Some very early standouts are: c=4 which produces 799 semiprimes; c=6 which produces 532 semiprimes; c=12 which produces only 431 semiprimes; c=18 which produces 364 semiprimes, and c=30 which produces only 320 semiprimes."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "24bb563c-9dc9-47a9-a35f-36dc3ad734fe",
                                                "sense_id": "42f89954-2596-3729-ab12-eee33625033a",
                                                "text": "2015, Jie Wang, Zachary A. Kissel, Introduction to Network Security: Theory and Practice, Wiley [under licence from Higher Education Press], page 113,\nFirstly, we should change semiprimes from time to time, where a particular semiprime should only be used in a time interval shorter than the time required to factor an RSA challenge number of a similar length. Secondly, we should use semiprimes that consist of more than 200 decimal digits."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ae8862d4-bc73-4ef0-997d-78e926497c43",
                                                "sense_id": "42f89954-2596-3729-ab12-eee33625033a",
                                                "text": "In this paper I will define four sequences of numbers obtained through concatenation, definitions which also use the notion of “sum of the digits of a number”, sequences that have the property to produce many primes, semiprimes and products of very few prime factors."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "semiprime",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "4050c68a-893d-309e-9c9a-c7ac701d02c5",
                                        "entry_id": "ade6ff11-7ae2-39af-942a-ae50c01c196b",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "That has properties derived directly or by extension from a semiprime."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "not-comparable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e94e27c7-cc5d-4d8a-9374-550f20b13f6c",
                                                "sense_id": "4050c68a-893d-309e-9c9a-c7ac701d02c5",
                                                "text": "The final part of the semiprime-semisimple analogy is given by\nProposition 4.4. A ring #92;mathbf#123;R#125; is semiprime if and only if #92;mathbf#123;R#125; is isomorphic to a subdirect product of prime rings."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3486b732-b094-4c52-8587-cc324a379fa2",
                                                "sense_id": "4050c68a-893d-309e-9c9a-c7ac701d02c5",
                                                "text": "In this chapter we shall study the structure of semiprime alternative algebras."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c6e80f7e-c6c1-486d-8e63-9cb7b02827de",
                                                "sense_id": "4050c68a-893d-309e-9c9a-c7ac701d02c5",
                                                "text": "Let f be a semiprime fuzzy ideal of S and x#92;inS."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
